  "query_preview": "..\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW:\nA small upper level trough is expected to remain over the area\nthrough Friday, and isolated to scattered severe thunderstorms are\npart of the story. Shortwave troughs that move through this flow\nwill aid in initiating the stronger storms. The main severe threat\nis for central Montana today, the eastern two thirds of the area\ntomorrow, and the eastern half of the area on Friday. A bit of\nCAPE is expected to be elevated above the boundary layer tonight,\nso some lightning and marginally strong storms are expected to\nmove through overnight tonight, especially in the central part of\nthe area. Any storm and rain reports are welcome.  \n\nBased on bulk shear from 0 to 6 kilometers and a small amount of\nsurface CAPE over central Montana on July Fourth, there is enough\nevidence there to suggest an isolated strong storm moving into the\nwestern counties in the afternoon. This is according to a few\ndeterministic GFS model soundings.\n\nThe GFS ensemble continues to suggest an upper ridge for next \nweek.\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\nNo deviations were made.\n\nThere is moderate to high confidence in showers and storms\noccurring now through Friday, with breaks at various times.\n\nThere is low to to moderate confidence on thunderstorm activity in\nthe afternoon of the Fourth of July.\n\nThere is moderate confidence in a ridge moving in next week, but \nlow confidence on this ridge suppressing all thunderstorm \nactivity.",
